<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlos Dávila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Carlos Dávila Espinoza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlos Dávila was a Chilean journalist, diplomat and politician. He helped overthrow President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-esteban-montero">Juan Esteban Montero</a> in June 1932, joining the junta of the Socialist Republic of Chile, then pushed out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marmaduke-grove">Marmaduke Grove</a> and ruled as provisional president for about three months. He later served as secretary general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlos-davila.jpg" alt="Carlos Dávila" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ministerio de Relaciones Exteriores de Chile. · CC BY 2.0 cl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlos_D%C3%A1vila_Espinoza_(1927).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 4 June 1932 the air force commander Marmaduke Grove and other officers, with the politicians <b>Carlos Dávila</b> and Eugenio Matte, overthrew President Juan Esteban Montero and proclaimed the Socialist Republic of Chile.</blockquote><a href="/state/socialist-republic-of-chile">Socialist Republic of Chile</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Los Ángeles, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-37.47,-72.35&amp;year=1931" aria-label="Los Ángeles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2741 3512 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carlos Dávila"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3191 3782h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3191 3782h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-37.47,-72.35&amp;year=1931">What was here in 1931? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
